1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And now, the old scout master.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,000
So it's nighttime at summer camp and the scouts are sitting around a picnic table, getting bitten by mosquitoes.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,000
So they decide they're going to move and they go to another picnic table and soon there's some fireflies floating around

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
and one of the boys says, well I give up, I'm going to bed.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Now the mosquitoes have flashlights and they're looking for them.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Hey this is podcast number 75.

7
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Hey this is Clark Green, welcome back to the Scout Master podcast.

8
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,000
So let's see here, looking in the mail bag.

9
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,000
We heard this week from Blaze Vitale, who is a new scout master out in Wisconsin.

10
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
He says, overall I've really enjoyed the time with my troop.

11
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,000
I feel like stepping up to become a scout master has given me more of a sense of purpose to my life

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
and it's going to positively influence my family, particularly my son.

13
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
The boys in the troop and the community.

14
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,000
I've learned lots from your podcasts and several of the books you've recommended.

15
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,000
The last podcast about summer camp was excellent.

16
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:44,000
I wrote out a synopsis of that section of the podcast and emailed it to all the parents who were coming.

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Your podcast consistently draws me back and continues to be my favorite.

18
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:53,000
I like the new website as well and the increased volume of post,

19
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,000
which have been coming out over the past several months.

20
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Great work, well thanks Blaze.

21
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,000
And I've been keeping up with Blaze over the past few months.

22
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,000
He's a new scout master as his message implied there.

23
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,000
And it's just great to follow that.

24
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Thomas Hulahan, assistant scout master with Troop 364 in Virginia Beach, Virginia,

25
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,000
was a winner of one of our latest caption contests.

26
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,000
And he wrote to say this, first off,

27
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
let me compliment you on your podcast simply outstanding.

28
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
The only shortcoming in my opinion is they're not long enough.

29
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Well, thanks Tom.

30
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Your knowledge, insight and humor is greatly appreciated by all that listen to you.

31
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:42,000
I am sure.

32
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
I was a highly decorated Weebelos leader.

33
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Did you get the Weebelos purple heart and all that?

34
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Yeah, me too.

35
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,000
And now I'm in my third year as an assistant scout master.

36
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The group of boys who bridged over with me are still at it.

37
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
And they're still with the program.

38
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,000
And we've done a good job recruiting and we have about 60 scouts now.

39
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
And we seem to continuously be growing.

40
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Once again, thank you for your dedication to scouting.

41
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Please keep up the good work.

42
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Well, thank you Tom.

43
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,000
It's good to hear that three years later things are still moving along strong 60 scouts.

44
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
That's a good size bunch.

45
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
And I appreciate you listening.

46
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:24,000
I appreciate you being in touch.

47
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,000
I'll remind you guys out there and girls how to keep in touch.

48
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:33,000
You can email me at scoutmastercg at Verizon.net

49
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
And you can check out the blog at scoutmastercg.com

50
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:44,000
And well, we'll let you know how to follow us on Facebook and Twitter and all that stuff before we leave today.

51
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Today on the podcast in scout master ship in seven minutes or less,

52
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
we're going to continue with our summer camp preparations.

53
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
And this time around, we're going to talk about how to prepare parents for summer camp.

54
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Not the ones who'll be going down and accompanying the scouts to summer camp,

55
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
but the ones who'll be staying home.

56
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Oh yeah, they need a little help too.

57
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:10,000
And then we'll move on to talking about the older boys in scouting

58
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:16,000
and exactly what we do and how we deal with that and the older boy question.

59
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:20,000
I'll warn you ahead of time, it's a little bit of a rant.

60
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,000
It's probably totally a rant.

61
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,000
So, you know, if you want to skip that part of the podcast, feel free.

62
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Hey, I think that's enough to do for a mid-summer podcast, don't you?

63
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Well, let's get started, shall we?

64
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Scout master ship in seven minutes or less.

65
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:47,000
So once we've got the scouts prepared to go to camp,

66
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,000
once we've prepared the troop, we've done our lists, we've sent our money,

67
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:57,000
we've done forms and we've scheduled adults to be at camp and all that kind of stuff

68
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,000
and figured out schedules of merit badges and all that business.

69
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,000
You know, we're ready to go,

70
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:08,000
but what we're doing is we're leaving a group of parents behind at home

71
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
and a lot of times we haven't prepared them at all.

72
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Now, this is usually going to concern mothers

73
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:21,000
and it's usually going to concern families whose scouts are going away to camp

74
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,000
for their first year.

75
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:24,000
That's just my experience.

76
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,000
I know that dads have a lot of the same concerns,

77
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
but I usually tend to hear these concerns more from mothers

78
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:38,000
or just kind of sense that there's some tension

79
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:43,000
or some reluctance or some questions about going away to camp

80
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,000
that mothers have, especially that first year

81
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:54,000
and we have not always done a very good job of preparing them for this.

82
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Parents themselves are pretty concentrated on getting the boys ready for camp

83
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,000
and they might be unaware of how much they're going to miss

84
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
and worry about their scout while he's away

85
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,000
or they may be aware of it and find it really kind of difficult to deal with.

86
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
They may feel uncomfortable turning over full-time care to people

87
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
they've just really gotten to know.

88
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
If we're talking about a boy going to summer camp for his first year,

89
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:27,000
he's been with a scout troop since maybe February

90
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,000
and has been on three, maybe four camp outs

91
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:37,000
and this may be enough to get to know the family pretty well

92
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:41,000
and know the boys pretty well and for the families to grow

93
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
and to trust the adult leadership of the troop,

94
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:48,000
but it's still a very big step.

95
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Sending your son away for an entire week

96
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:57,000
and it may be the first time he's been away for that long from home

97
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:01,000
and it's not necessarily people you know really, really well

98
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
and that you've known for years and years.

99
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:07,000
And parents worry about the kind of food that they'll be eating

100
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:11,000
and who'll be looking after the daily concerns of clean clothes

101
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:15,000
and maybe taking a shower every once in a while.

102
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:19,000
They'll be worried if the other boys will tease their son

103
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
or if their son will be able to find his way around camp.

104
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:26,000
They worry about a lot of things.

105
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:30,000
And what happens if he doesn't like being at camp?

106
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
Can he call home?

107
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,000
Can I call him?

108
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,000
How am I going to know what's going on?

109
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
It's an uncertain time.

110
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
I see this anxiety in the parents of scouts headed for camp

111
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,000
for the first year all the time.

112
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
Some of them find the experience a lot more difficult

113
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
than their sons ever will.

114
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
They don't find a whole lot of solace in the first letter

115
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
that they get from their scouts either.

116
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:01,000
Hey, I am having good time at camp. See you later.

117
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Or hey, I hate camp. I'll see you later.

118
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:11,000
Or yeah, John broke his arm. See you later.

119
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:14,000
You know, there's not a whole lot communicated there

120
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:17,000
and they're not going to necessarily get a letter back to him

121
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
in reply to that one within a week's time,

122
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
depending on where the camp is.

123
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
You understand what I'm saying?

124
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:27,000
You probably will remember this from the first year

125
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
that your son went away to camp.

126
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,000
But you know, if you're listening to this podcast,

127
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
it's pretty likely that you went with him.

128
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
But these are real things.

129
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,000
How can we as scout leaders help parents prepare

130
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:41,000
for how they're going to feel once they've dropped their sons off

131
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
and they return home for, you know,

132
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:47,000
what could be a very quiet and a very long week?

133
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:50,000
Being separated from a child for this length of time

134
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
is a challenge for every parent,

135
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:56,000
even though many other parents have gone through it before.

136
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:00,000
So here's some thoughts that I think will help parents

137
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,000
be prepared for their sons going off to camp

138
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
for the first time.

139
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
And, you know, the first one's pretty easy.

140
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:10,000
It's acknowledged that this causes most parents

141
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
at least some distress.

142
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
And it's really a normal part of a child growing up.

143
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:18,000
It's temporary, even though it might feel like

144
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
a week is a long time,

145
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
and you're going to feel better.

146
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:24,000
Yeah, you will.

147
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
It's stressful.

148
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,000
It's stressful for a parent.

149
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,000
It can be very difficult.

150
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:33,000
It can be a great relief, you know,

151
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
for the scouts to go away for a week,

152
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
but they're still going to be worried

153
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
and they're still going to be a little stressed.

154
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
Now, most scouts are going to encounter some homesickness

155
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
and some ups and downs.

156
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:48,000
We're going to talk about how to deal with homesickness

157
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
in our next podcast,

158
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,000
but, you know, we understand,

159
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
as experienced leaders,

160
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
that most scouts are going to encounter some homesickness

161
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
the first year that they go away

162
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
and sometimes the second and third year too.

163
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
And they're going to have some ups and downs.

164
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
They're going to enjoy some parts of camp

165
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
and not enjoy some others.

166
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
They're going to be some challenges.

167
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
What we can help parents understand

168
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
is that his fellow scouts and the adult leaders

169
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
that will be at camp with him

170
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
and the camp staff are all working hard

171
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
to make sure that it's overall a positive experience,

172
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
that it's a measured challenge

173
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,000
that scouts can overcome,

174
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
and they do that year after year after year.

175
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:30,000
We can let parents know that we expect some homesickness.

176
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
We expect it.

177
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
And we expect other difficulties to arise

178
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
and we're prepared to deal with them.

179
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
And that they also

180
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
may be a little difficult,

181
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
they may be a little distasteful for a scout,

182
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
but they're a part of a healthy, growing process

183
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
that all scouts are going to encounter in one way or another.

184
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
I don't like to make a big issue of it

185
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
because sometimes it can be a little distracting,

186
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
but, you know, every one of our children,

187
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
one of these days

188
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
is going to pack their things

189
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
and going to head out

190
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
and make their own way in the world.

191
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
And if they have

192
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
the experience of going away to summer camp

193
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
before they're going to pack their bags

194
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
and move out of the house

195
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
and end up at college

196
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
or end up going off on their own,

197
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
you know, it's going to be a positive

198
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
experience for them and they're going to learn some things.

199
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
I know that when my son

200
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
went away to college

201
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
and he came home

202
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
for that first

203
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
break in the fall,

204
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
he was absolutely stunned

205
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
at how naive

206
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
and unprepared most of his fellow

207
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
freshman students were

208
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
that he shared a dorm with

209
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
for the college experience.

210
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
And they were unprepared,

211
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
I mean, he was amazed that

212
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
very few of them knew how to do laundry

213
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
or knew how to look after themselves.

214
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
We're able to survive,

215
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
you know, about very basic

216
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
day-to-day things.

217
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
He had

218
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
been, let's see,

219
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
to summer camp for

220
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
three or four years

221
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
for a week at a time

222
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
as a boy

223
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
and then joined the camp staff for

224
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
I guess he was there for about four years

225
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
full-time during the summers.

226
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
And so, going away to college

227
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
he was ready.

228
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
He knew how to look after himself.

229
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
He knew how to deal with any

230
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
feelings of homesickness

231
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
or anything like that. He knew how to make friends.

232
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
He knew how to work with groups of people.

233
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
So, I try

234
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
and let parents know that this is a very positive

235
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
experience, even though there might be

236
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
some stress and some negatives involved with it

237
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
it's overall a very

238
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
positive experience for a scout.

239
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
So we want to encourage parents to think

240
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
positively about the time that

241
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
the boys are going to be spending at camp. Scouts

242
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
typically thrive at summer camp

243
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
and return home very happy.

244
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
They may have had a rough time here and there, but

245
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
when it's all over, they're happy

246
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
scouts.

247
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
I would say that in the run up to summer camp

248
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
instead of

249
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
discussing and sharing your worries as a parent

250
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
with your scout

251
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
or how much you're going to miss him

252
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
talk about all the great new experiences

253
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
that he's going to have

254
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
when he goes away to camp. Ask all

255
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
the questions of all the adult

256
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
leaders that you need to ask.

257
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
There are really no silly questions. No.

258
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
And

259
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
hopefully you don't get an attitude

260
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
from any adult

261
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
because it's better to ask than to worry.

262
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
You know, if you need to ask about the food

263
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
if you need to ask about the shower

264
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
facilities, if you need to ask about the campsite

265
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
if you need to ask about

266
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
what they'll be doing

267
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
definitely ask.

268
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
And then understand

269
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
as a parent and respect

270
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
the rules and times for

271
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
helping off and picking up

272
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
and visiting camp.

273
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
Some places in the country

274
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
camps pretty good distance

275
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
and parents aren't going to be visiting.

276
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
Some places have

277
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
a regular schedule

278
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
where there's a day usually late in the week

279
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
where parents come and visit and see what the boys

280
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
have been up to. But understand

281
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
and respect those rules.

282
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
I think that's really, really

283
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
important for parents to know.

284
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
Also, understand and respect

285
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
how you'll be communicating

286
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
with your scout

287
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
and how he'll be communicating with home.

288
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
For the most part

289
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
I like to see the communication

290
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
be

291
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
very limited

292
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
and I like to see it be

293
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
under the

294
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
oversight of the adult

295
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
leaders that are there at camp.

296
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
And that can be really rough for a parent

297
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
and so understanding how

298
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
that's going to work up ahead of time

299
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
is going to be important for them.

300
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
And I would lay that out

301
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
I would make sure as a scout leader that I've laid

302
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
that out very clearly.

303
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
I encourage parents to plan something

304
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
interesting or special to do while their

305
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
boys away at camp. You know, make some

306
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
plans for that week. Don't just sit around

307
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
and worry about the boy. Those are

308
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
just a few of the issues

309
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
that we are working with in preparing

310
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
parents for summer camp.

311
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
It's one of the things that often gets overlooked

312
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
in

313
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
the hundred of other details

314
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
that you're taking on.

315
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
And keep your eyes open because anxious

316
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
parents are very easy to spot

317
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
and make sure that you've answered

318
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
all the questions that they need to have answered

319
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
and that they're going to feel

320
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
as secure as possible

321
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
about sending their scout away to camp for the first

322
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
year. And you know

323
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
it's almost always

324
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
I would say 99.99%

325
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
of the time in my experience

326
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
it's a positive

327
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
experience for the parents

328
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
and for the scout.

329
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
Music

330
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
