362
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
Music

363
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
One of the fun things about having a blog is

364
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
you post something out there and then

365
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
a discussion begins

366
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
in the comments section of the post

367
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
and you get a lot of interesting ideas

368
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
reflections and perspectives on things

369
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
and one of the recent posts on the blog

370
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
was about whether or not

371
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
parents should push their

372
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
sons to stay in scouting

373
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
and one of the ideas that came out of that

374
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
was talking about

375
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
the retention of older boys

376
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
you know boys who were 15, 16, 17

377
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
and

378
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
it was suggested

379
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
that

380
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
if scouting wants to keep boys that are that age

381
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
they have to give them age-appropriate

382
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
programs apart from the troop program

383
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
and

384
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
that was the only way that we're going to keep them

385
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
as a part of scouts.

386
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
Here comes the rant

387
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
that I warned you about earlier

388
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
but I just think that is just total malarkey

389
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
I don't believe it at all

390
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
I've heard about it for the past 25 years

391
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
and I got to tell you it's just not

392
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
been my experience

393
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
we talk about it at the troop level

394
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
you know in training programs

395
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
and things like that

396
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
there's this constant

397
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
at a 5

398
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
at a district to council level

399
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
especially at our summer camp

400
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
about the older boy programs

401
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
and we have to have these otherwise

402
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
they're just going to walk away from scouting

403
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
there's the talk about that

404
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
and the venture program and everything

405
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
oh my gosh we have to have

406
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
the whole scout program is geared for

407
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
the younger boys in the troop

408
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
and the older boys they just lose interest

409
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:23,000
and they leave

410
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
but this is actually what happens in our troop

411
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
boys on average

412
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,000
of that 35 I would say

413
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
about a half of them

414
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
are 15, 16, 17 years old

415
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
so

416
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
somewhere between 10 and 15 of them

417
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
let's say

418
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
and of that 10 or 15

419
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
we lose about 4 of them

420
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
3 to 4 of them

421
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
every year

422
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
and the reason that we lose them

423
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
is because they turn 18

424
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
and they graduate from high school

425
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
and that's why we lose them

426
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
we don't lose them because they quit scouts

427
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
we don't lose them because

428
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
they stop showing up

429
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
and they don't retrader

430
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
we lose them because

431
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,000
they turn 18 and they're too old to be scouts

432
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
now

433
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
in the interest of full disclosure

434
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
and complete honesty

435
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
I think probably of that group of boys

436
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
who are 15, 16, 17 years old

437
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
1 to 2

438
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
we might just lose over

439
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
the attrition of them finding

440
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
something else to do and not being interested

441
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
in scouts anymore

442
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
now that's not an older boy problem

443
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
that's just

444
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
not an older boy problem

445
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
I guess

446
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
and I hear from people

447
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
who have a scout troop

448
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
who somehow

449
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
either perceive or actually lose

450
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
a lot of older boys over time

451
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
and I have the antidote for it

452
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
if you do have an older boy problem

453
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
I hear an awful lot about them

454
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
so there's got to be some out there

455
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
I have a quick two step program

456
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
for you to eliminate your older boy

457
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
problem

458
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
step number one

459
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
use the patrol method and make sure

460
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
that scouts are leading their own troop

461
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
I know everybody's tired of hearing about it

462
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
I know

463
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
but if you want to retain older scouts

464
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
have them lead their own troop

465
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
step number two

466
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
let them set their own level of participation

467
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
don't tell them

468
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
how much they have to participate

469
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
okay

470
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:25,000
for every highly engaged older scout

471
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
in a key leader position

472
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
in our troop

473
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
I have one who is less active

474
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
and less engaged because

475
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
he's doing other stuff too

476
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
he's playing football

477
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
or he's on some academic team

478
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
or he's following a very strong

479
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
academic course in school

480
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
and

481
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
you know

482
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
he is less active

483
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
than his fellow scouts

484
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
but I don't get angry with him

485
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
and I don't put policies in place

486
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
designed to make him feel bad

487
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
with certain percentages

488
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
of things that he must do

489
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
or something like that

490
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
not every boy is going to step into

491
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
a senior leadership position

492
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
some of them are going

493
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
to be involved to a certain point

494
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
and that's what they want to do

495
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
and that's it

496
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
and if you're not prepared for that to happen

497
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
you're going to have an older boy problem

498
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
I can guarantee you

499
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
now

500
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
as a result

501
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
we retain a lot of those older boys

502
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
they remain active

503
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
all the way until they graduate

504
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
from high school

505
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
that's the way that it works

506
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
now one thing that we do have

507
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
that is kind of a natural outgrowth

508
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
of the things

509
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
that scouts do

510
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
is we do have one annual trip

511
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
that is a venture patrol trip

512
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
for boys

513
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
from 13

514
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
up

515
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
and usually that's a canoe trip

516
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
this year it happens to be a trip

517
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
to Kandersteg International Scout Center

518
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
in Switzerland

519
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,000
but we do have that

520
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,000
and that I guess you could call

521
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
an older boy program

522
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
but the real older boy program

523
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
is that they

524
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
lead their troop

525
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
they lead it

526
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,000
every time you meet

527
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
they lead it when we go to summer camp

528
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
they lead it when we go camping

529
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
they lead it at the venture patrol trips

530
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
I mean come on folks

531
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
that's what keeps them interested

532
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
and challenged and engaged

533
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
there is

534
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,000
no older boy problem

535
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:35,000
when you run the patrol method

536
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
and scouts lead their own

537
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,000
troop

538
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
so if you're having an attrition problem

539
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:43,000
with boys who are 15, 16

540
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
and 17 years old

541
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
then you need to go back

542
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
and you need to look at the way your troop is being run

543
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
because

544
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:53,000
my guess

545
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:55,000
the boys aren't running it

546
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
and you need to look at

547
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
the way that you're making a guy feel

548
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,000
if he's truly interested in sports

549
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
or academics or something else

550
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:05,000
outside of scouts

551
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:07,000
and he feels as though he's penalized

552
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:09,000
for being interested in things outside of scouts

553
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:11,000
and doing other things in his community

554
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:13,000
those are the two things that

555
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:15,000
